WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.066 --> 00:00:00.767
Jake, first off,

00:00:00.767 --> 00:00:01.901
what's on this tape

00:00:01.901 --> 00:00:04.270
and why is it seen as so critical

00:00:04.504 --> 00:00:05.705
a piece of evidence

00:00:05.705 --> 00:00:07.874
for the special counsel

00:00:08.908 --> 00:00:10.410
so CNN reporters

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:11.077
Paula Reid,

00:00:11.077 --> 00:00:12.612
Caitlin Palance and Kaitlan Collins

00:00:12.612 --> 00:00:13.546
broke this story.

00:00:13.546 --> 00:00:15.648
And what it is, is an audiotape

00:00:15.648 --> 00:00:17.817
that the special counsel, Jack Smith, has

00:00:19.085 --> 00:00:21.988
from July 2021.

00:00:21.988 --> 00:00:23.990
President Trump, former President Trump

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:26.192
at Bedminster, his resort in New Jersey,

00:00:26.459 --> 00:00:27.927
a meeting with some individuals

00:00:27.927 --> 00:00:29.262
that are writing an autobiography

00:00:29.262 --> 00:00:29.729
of his former

00:00:29.729 --> 00:00:30.830
chief of staff, Mark Meadows.

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:33.366
Now on the tape, we are told.

00:00:33.366 --> 00:00:34.934
Donald Trump is pushing back

00:00:34.934 --> 00:00:36.436
on an article

00:00:36.436 --> 00:00:37.604
in which Mark Milley

00:00:37.604 --> 00:00:38.571
the chairman of the Joint

00:00:38.571 --> 00:00:39.539
Chiefs of Staff,

00:00:39.539 --> 00:00:41.474
is described as keeping Trump

00:00:41.474 --> 00:00:42.575
from attacking Iran.

00:00:42.575 --> 00:00:44.744
And Trump is angry about this

00:00:44.744 --> 00:00:46.413
and basically holds up a document.

00:00:46.413 --> 00:00:49.682
And we hear the rustling of papers.

00:00:49.682 --> 00:00:50.183
We're not sure

00:00:50.183 --> 00:00:51.818
if it's actually the document question,

00:00:51.818 --> 00:00:54.354
but he refers to a classified document

00:00:54.354 --> 00:00:56.256
that would disprove all of this.

00:00:56.256 --> 00:00:57.690
But he says he can't share it

00:00:57.690 --> 00:00:58.792
because it's classified.

00:00:58.792 --> 00:01:00.560
Now, why this is significant

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:01.327
is because

00:01:01.327 --> 00:01:03.029
that would seem to suggest

00:01:03.029 --> 00:01:05.198
that Donald Trump knew

00:01:05.198 --> 00:01:06.866
that just because he brought documents

00:01:06.866 --> 00:01:08.268
with him from the White House

00:01:08.268 --> 00:01:09.869
to any one of his resorts

00:01:09.869 --> 00:01:12.572
didn't automatically declassify them

00:01:13.173 --> 00:01:15.108
and that he didn't have the ability

00:01:15.108 --> 00:01:16.409
to declassify them

00:01:16.409 --> 00:01:17.510
just by thinking about them,

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:19.412
as he claimed on Fox.

00:01:19.412 --> 00:01:20.580
And it seems to contradict

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:22.082
what he has said about this

00:01:22.082 --> 00:01:23.850
publicly as well,

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:25.652
including to our own Kaitlan Collins

00:01:25.652 --> 00:01:27.120
at the town hall a few weeks ago.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:27.887
Let's roll that tape

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:31.825
You have the Presidential Records Act.

00:01:31.825 --> 00:01:32.659
I was there

00:01:32.659 --> 00:01:34.561
and I took what I took,

00:01:34.561 --> 00:01:35.862
and it gets declassified.

00:01:35.862 --> 00:01:36.629
Did you ever show

00:01:36.629 --> 00:01:38.731
those classified documents to anyone?

00:01:38.731 --> 00:01:39.399
Not really.

00:01:39.399 --> 00:01:40.633
I would have the right to.

00:01:40.633 --> 00:01:41.801
By the way, they would dismiss

00:01:41.801 --> 00:01:43.069
if I do after.

00:01:43.269 --> 00:01:44.671
Not that I can think of.

00:01:44.671 --> 00:01:45.839
Let me just tell you,

00:01:45.839 --> 00:01:47.440
I have the absolute right to do whatever

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:48.875
I want with them.

00:01:53.113 --> 00:01:55.281
So that would seem to contradict that.

00:01:55.315 --> 00:01:56.883
Now, we are going to talk

00:01:56.883 --> 00:01:59.819
to Donald Trump's former attorney,

00:02:00.220 --> 00:02:02.889
Tim Parks, Hillary, about this.

00:02:03.623 --> 00:02:04.958
Coming up on the lead,

00:02:04.958 --> 00:02:06.793
he was with the Trump legal team

00:02:06.793 --> 00:02:08.228
until just a couple of weeks ago.

00:02:08.228 --> 00:02:10.029
So we're going to talk about this

00:02:10.029 --> 00:02:12.699
and see what his response is, Brianna.

00:02:13.233 --> 00:02:13.533
Yeah,

00:02:13.533 --> 00:02:15.201
and that's going to be so interesting.

00:02:15.201 --> 00:02:17.437
Will be to stay tuned for that

00:02:17.704 --> 00:02:19.839
on the lead coming up here at 4:00.

00:02:19.873 --> 00:02:20.773
Jake Tapper,

00:02:20.773 --> 00:02:23.176
thank you so much for being with us, Jim.

00:02:23.176 --> 00:02:23.443
All right.

00:02:23.443 --> 00:02:24.777
So legal concerns aside,

00:02:24.777 --> 00:02:26.112
what kind of national security

00:02:26.112 --> 00:02:28.581
risk is it for a document such as this

00:02:29.149 --> 00:02:31.117
to be potentially out in the open

00:02:31.117 --> 00:02:31.851
like that?

00:02:31.851 --> 00:02:33.653
CNN military analyst, retired Lieutenant

00:02:33.653 --> 00:02:35.421
General Mark Hertling joins us now.

00:02:35.421 --> 00:02:37.724
So what we know of this document?

00:02:37.724 --> 00:02:41.327
General is that detailed plans

00:02:41.895 --> 00:02:44.264
in Iran, attack plan.

00:02:44.731 --> 00:02:46.232
Classified material.

00:02:46.232 --> 00:02:46.633
You've dealt

00:02:46.633 --> 00:02:48.001
with classified material commanding

00:02:48.001 --> 00:02:49.636
forces in the region before.

00:02:49.636 --> 00:02:51.104
What is the national security risk

00:02:51.104 --> 00:02:53.273
of having that out

00:02:53.273 --> 00:02:55.175
outside of a secure location?

00:02:57.443 --> 00:02:57.744
Well,

00:02:57.744 --> 00:02:58.711
first, Jim, I'm glad

00:02:58.711 --> 00:02:59.546
you started off

00:02:59.546 --> 00:03:00.980
by saying outside

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:02.916
of the legal implications of all this,

00:03:02.916 --> 00:03:05.018
because that is a whole different story.

00:03:05.251 --> 00:03:07.854
What I'm most concerned about as a user

00:03:08.021 --> 00:03:09.789
of classified information

00:03:09.789 --> 00:03:11.891
is how much this gives our enemies,

00:03:12.225 --> 00:03:13.359
how much

00:03:13.359 --> 00:03:14.827
the potential is

00:03:14.827 --> 00:03:16.262
for not only waving

00:03:16.262 --> 00:03:16.963
the stock

00:03:16.963 --> 00:03:19.232
immense around in a room with reporters

00:03:19.232 --> 00:03:20.633
who are writing a book,

00:03:20.633 --> 00:03:22.235
but also the potential

00:03:22.235 --> 00:03:23.069
for the president

00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:25.138
to just have these documents

00:03:25.138 --> 00:03:27.840
in a friggin golf clubhouse.

00:03:28.341 --> 00:03:28.908
You know,

00:03:28.908 --> 00:03:29.509
that's the thing

00:03:29.509 --> 00:03:30.910
that just is amazing to me.

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:32.011
This is nowhere near

00:03:32.011 --> 00:03:33.613
a skiff location

00:03:33.613 --> 00:03:35.815
where classified documents are held.

00:03:36.049 --> 00:03:37.750
He's sharing it with others,

00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:39.185
in this case reporters.

00:03:39.185 --> 00:03:40.954
But who else has he shared it with?

00:03:40.954 --> 00:03:42.522
And what does it have

00:03:42.522 --> 00:03:44.123
inside of these paperwork?

00:03:44.123 --> 00:03:45.792
You know, allegedly?

00:03:45.792 --> 00:03:47.126
It has to do with

00:03:48.294 --> 00:03:49.262
snapping back

00:03:49.262 --> 00:03:50.396
at General Mark Milley

00:03:50.396 --> 00:03:52.699
because of some things he recommended

00:03:52.865 --> 00:03:56.469
in terms of an Iranian contingency plan.

00:03:56.736 --> 00:03:59.672
If it was that this is dialog between

00:03:59.872 --> 00:04:01.040
the chairman of the Joint

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:03.343
Chiefs, the senior military adviser,

00:04:03.509 --> 00:04:06.546
giving potential recommendations

00:04:06.546 --> 00:04:08.748
for use of military force

00:04:08.748 --> 00:04:10.950
against a potential opponent.

00:04:11.217 --> 00:04:13.052
Now, friends are interested in that,

00:04:13.052 --> 00:04:14.587
and much more importantly,

00:04:14.587 --> 00:04:16.256
enemies are interested in that.

00:04:16.256 --> 00:04:17.190
So if this document

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:18.324
is just lying around, it's

00:04:18.324 --> 00:04:18.858
just it's

00:04:18.858 --> 00:04:20.326
just indescribable,

00:04:20.326 --> 00:04:21.594
this probably bad

00:04:21.594 --> 00:04:23.096
and as far as I'm concerned.

00:04:23.096 --> 00:04:24.831
So let me ask you,

00:04:24.831 --> 00:04:26.432
especially given you've commanded forces

00:04:26.432 --> 00:04:28.001
in the region under threat,

00:04:28.001 --> 00:04:28.901
how would U.S.

00:04:28.901 --> 00:04:31.037
service members deployed in the region

00:04:31.371 --> 00:04:33.206
feel about that sort of information

00:04:33.206 --> 00:04:34.307
not being protected,

00:04:34.307 --> 00:04:36.109
given that information

00:04:36.109 --> 00:04:37.410
could conceivably impact

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:38.144
their own safety?

00:04:40.046 --> 00:04:40.580
Yeah, well, it

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:41.648
signals a move

00:04:41.648 --> 00:04:42.615
or many moves

00:04:42.615 --> 00:04:45.318
or contingency operations or plans

00:04:45.952 --> 00:04:46.252
You know,

00:04:46.252 --> 00:04:48.421
when when the president was asking

00:04:48.421 --> 00:04:49.489
General Milley

00:04:49.489 --> 00:04:50.023
to help,

00:04:50.023 --> 00:04:50.823
as outlined

00:04:50.823 --> 00:04:52.325
in some of the articles written up

00:04:52.325 --> 00:04:54.527
during that period of time,

00:04:54.527 --> 00:04:54.861
you know,

00:04:54.861 --> 00:04:56.162
the chairman of the Joint Chiefs

00:04:56.162 --> 00:04:58.331
comes back with different options.

00:04:58.631 --> 00:05:00.066
All of the options

00:05:00.066 --> 00:05:02.168
put Americans in harm's way,

00:05:02.168 --> 00:05:03.503
in harm's way.

00:05:03.503 --> 00:05:04.537
So if you're a member

00:05:04.537 --> 00:05:06.072
of a military organization

00:05:06.072 --> 00:05:08.441
and you know, your commander in chief

00:05:08.708 --> 00:05:10.476
possibly has information

00:05:10.476 --> 00:05:12.111
that he's not securing,

00:05:12.111 --> 00:05:13.980
it's just very dangerous

00:05:13.980 --> 00:05:15.682
for the military operations.

00:05:15.682 --> 00:05:17.483
It's not something

00:05:17.483 --> 00:05:19.585
that we've ever experienced before

00:05:19.952 --> 00:05:20.420
in terms

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:21.387
of a commander in chief

00:05:21.387 --> 00:05:22.121
giving away

00:05:22.121 --> 00:05:24.223
the kind of potential contingencies

00:05:24.390 --> 00:05:25.191
or options

00:05:25.191 --> 00:05:27.060
that he has to

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:28.127
to actually deploy

00:05:28.127 --> 00:05:29.195
troops in different areas.

00:05:30.296 --> 00:05:31.831
So another thing we've

00:05:31.831 --> 00:05:33.366
learned from the ongoing

00:05:33.366 --> 00:05:35.034
repercussions of the discord

00:05:35.034 --> 00:05:35.935
leak, this airman

00:05:35.935 --> 00:05:37.170
who was sharing

00:05:37.170 --> 00:05:38.137
classified documents

00:05:38.137 --> 00:05:40.373
on discord, the Washington Post reports

00:05:40.373 --> 00:05:41.341
the Pentagon

00:05:41.341 --> 00:05:43.176
documents from that leak showed

00:05:43.176 --> 00:05:44.844
that Iran plans to increase

00:05:44.844 --> 00:05:45.812
attacks on U.S.

00:05:45.812 --> 00:05:48.181
troops in Syria.

00:05:48.181 --> 00:05:50.616
I wonder, because we've been watching

00:05:50.616 --> 00:05:52.185
the position of Iran in the region chain.

00:05:52.185 --> 00:05:53.786
We have some of our allies

00:05:53.786 --> 00:05:55.355
reestablishing diplomatic connections

00:05:55.355 --> 00:05:56.756
with Iran.

00:05:57.123 --> 00:05:58.091
What is the state

00:05:58.091 --> 00:05:59.926
of the US-Iranian relationship there?

00:05:59.926 --> 00:06:01.427
And has the chances

00:06:01.427 --> 00:06:03.429
of a direct confrontation increased

00:06:03.429 --> 00:06:05.431
given a revelation such as this

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:09.035
I think what we're talking about here

00:06:09.035 --> 00:06:09.669
in this case

00:06:09.669 --> 00:06:10.370
with the Washington

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:11.137
Post article

00:06:11.137 --> 00:06:13.106
and the potential leaks through the text

00:06:13.106 --> 00:06:14.173
here, accounts

00:06:14.173 --> 00:06:17.243
is not just an engagement with Iran.

00:06:17.243 --> 00:06:18.177
Directly,

00:06:18.177 --> 00:06:19.912
but with the paramilitary units

00:06:19.912 --> 00:06:23.149
that they use in various regions.

00:06:23.316 --> 00:06:24.851
In this case, Syria.

00:06:24.851 --> 00:06:26.419
The thing that got my attention

00:06:26.419 --> 00:06:27.553
truthfully Jake, in this

00:06:27.553 --> 00:06:29.155
in this article by The Washington Post

00:06:29.155 --> 00:06:29.889
this morning

00:06:29.889 --> 00:06:31.023
was an increase

00:06:31.023 --> 00:06:31.791
in the use

00:06:31.791 --> 00:06:32.291
of something

00:06:32.291 --> 00:06:33.393
called EFP

00:06:33.393 --> 00:06:35.762
as explosively formed penetrators.

00:06:36.062 --> 00:06:37.830
This is a type of improvised

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:39.198
explosive device

00:06:39.198 --> 00:06:41.834
that truthfully, our troops, my troops,

00:06:41.834 --> 00:06:43.102
when we were in Iraq,

00:06:43.102 --> 00:06:45.138
experienced as Iranian

00:06:45.138 --> 00:06:47.974
militia came across the border into Iraq

00:06:47.974 --> 00:06:48.841
and use these

00:06:48.841 --> 00:06:50.977
these are extremely dangerous weapons.

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:52.512
They're much more deadly

00:06:52.512 --> 00:06:53.846
than a typical IED.

00:06:53.846 --> 00:06:55.114
They can penetrate

00:06:55.114 --> 00:06:56.883
a couple of inches of armor

00:06:56.883 --> 00:06:58.084
and we

00:06:58.084 --> 00:06:59.719
truthfully, during a period of time

00:06:59.719 --> 00:07:01.187
when Iranian militias

00:07:01.187 --> 00:07:03.523
were using these fees in Iraq,

00:07:03.523 --> 00:07:06.125
we saw more casualties than we had

00:07:06.359 --> 00:07:08.227
just simply from IED.

00:07:08.227 --> 00:07:09.595
So the fact that they could be

00:07:09.595 --> 00:07:11.130
using them in Syria

00:07:11.130 --> 00:07:13.533
and using them against the Kurds is

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:15.768
and against the type of equipment

00:07:15.768 --> 00:07:17.737
the US and Kurdish

00:07:17.737 --> 00:07:19.739
partners have in that area

00:07:19.739 --> 00:07:21.174
is extremely dangerous.

00:07:21.174 --> 00:07:22.074
I believe

00:07:22.074 --> 00:07:23.776
the US put the number of more than 600

00:07:23.776 --> 00:07:25.945
dead blamed on exactly those devices.

00:07:25.945 --> 00:07:27.480
Those IEDs interact.

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:27.914
Just quickly,

00:07:27.914 --> 00:07:29.449
can the MRAPs,

00:07:29.449 --> 00:07:31.050
those vehicles, can they withstand

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:33.152
a blast from an EFP?

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:35.188
Yeah, yeah.

00:07:35.188 --> 00:07:36.656
That's the interesting part.

00:07:36.656 --> 00:07:38.124
They they can't.

00:07:38.124 --> 00:07:39.992
In some cases they can jam.

00:07:39.992 --> 00:07:40.693
But truthfully,

00:07:40.693 --> 00:07:41.627
the MRAPs

00:07:41.627 --> 00:07:44.831
were designed to take blast from below

00:07:45.431 --> 00:07:48.768
as a typical IED and deflect the blast.

00:07:48.901 --> 00:07:50.570
What the EFP does

00:07:50.570 --> 00:07:53.206
is it forms a penetration of molten

00:07:53.673 --> 00:07:55.074
copper or led

00:07:55.074 --> 00:07:56.642
that can pierce the IEDs

00:07:56.642 --> 00:07:58.344
and then rumble around inside.

00:07:58.344 --> 00:07:59.579
It's a very dangerous thing

00:07:59.579 --> 00:08:01.047
and it can penetrate any armor.

00:08:01.047 --> 00:08:01.347
In fact,

00:08:01.347 --> 00:08:02.048
I've seen it

00:08:02.048 --> 00:08:04.984
penetrate parts of M-1 tanks as well.

00:08:05.551 --> 00:08:06.419
Goodness.

00:08:06.652 --> 00:08:08.120
Deadly, indeed.

00:08:08.120 --> 00:08:08.821
General Hertling,

00:08:08.821 --> 00:08:09.789
always good to have you on.

00:08:09.789 --> 00:08:10.456
Thanks so much.

